Pouch utimensat sets times over SYS_WSTAT; chmod's refusal is EPERM (G12, operator votes 17 and 18, 2026-10-22).

utimensat issued Linux's SYS_utimensat, which the seam retargets to the
0xFFFF sentinel (0001), so every time setter answered ENOSYS. musl routes
futimens, futimes, utimes, futimesat, lutimes and utime through utimensat,
so touch, cp -p, tar and make's own stamps all failed with it. SYS_WSTAT (59)
now carries four time bits that mirror 9P's (vote 18): ATIME and MTIME alone
take the file server's clock; with ATIME_SET or MTIME_SET the time is the
caller's, read from the struct timespec[2] at x6 (atime, then mtime). Linux
on aarch64 has no seven-argument call, so the seam gains __syscall7; musl's
own __SYSCALL_NARGS already dispatches seven.

Each time is UTIME_OMIT (left out of the mask), UTIME_NOW (its bit alone) or
a value (its bit and its _SET bit). A nanosecond that is neither and lies
outside 0..999999999 is EINVAL before anything traps. Null times is both at
UTIME_NOW. Both at UTIME_OMIT answers 0 and reads no path, descriptor or
flag, as Linux does. The seconds cross by value; the kernel judges them.

A null path with a real descriptor is the descriptor form: no open, and any
flag is EINVAL, as Linux's do_utimes_fd has it. Otherwise the path opens
O_PATH as fchmodat's does, O_NOFOLLOW under AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW, which sets a
link's own times. AT_EMPTY_PATH with "" names the descriptor itself, or the
working directory for AT_FDCWD (Linux's LOOKUP_EMPTY). Any other flag is
EINVAL, a null path EFAULT, a real dirfd with a name ENOTSUP (P-3).

The kernel answers EACCES for every refusal, because -EPERM is -1, the flat
error the seam reads as EIO (0001). Every time request but the touch (both
times to the server's clock) is the owner's or CAP_HOSTOWNER's alone, so its
refusal is POSIX's EPERM, set directly as link sets it (0062); the touch also
admits write permission, and its refusal stays EACCES, as POSIX has it.
chmod's one refusal is ownership too, so fchmod and fchmodat answer the
wstat's EACCES as EPERM; fchmodat's open (a component's search denied) and
lchmod's fstat keep their EACCES.

--- a/arch/aarch64/syscall_arch.h
+++ b/arch/aarch64/syscall_arch.h
@@ -1,7 +1,7 @@
 /*
  * pouch: the Thylacine syscall seam.
  *
- * musl's __syscall0..6 issue `svc 0` with the syscall number in x8. The
+ * musl's __syscall0..7 issue `svc 0` with the syscall number in x8. The
  * number comes from arch/aarch64/bits/syscall.h.in, which pouch retargets
  * from Linux values to Thylacine SYS_* values. A musl syscall macro with
  * no Thylacine equivalent is retargeted to POUCH_SYSCALL_UNIMPL; the guard
@@ -11,6 +11,9 @@
  *   P-3 - an un-retargeted POSIX call is a clean ENOSYS, never silently
  *         wrong and never a stray `svc` the kernel rejects with a flat -1.
  *
+ * __syscall7 is Thylacine's alone: Linux on aarch64 has no seven-argument
+ * call, and SYS_WSTAT reads its times pointer from x6.
+ *
  * POUCH_SYSCALL_UNIMPL must equal the sentinel literal used in
  * arch/aarch64/bits/syscall.h.in.
  */
@@ -96,6 +99,20 @@
 	register long x4 __asm__("x4") = e;
 	register long x5 __asm__("x5") = f;
 	__asm_syscall("r"(x8), "0"(x0), "r"(x1), "r"(x2), "r"(x3), "r"(x4), "r"(x5));
+}
+
+static inline long __syscall7(long n, long a, long b, long c, long d, long e, long f, long g)
+{
+	if (n == POUCH_SYSCALL_UNIMPL) return -ENOSYS;
+	register long x8 __asm__("x8") = n;
+	register long x0 __asm__("x0") = a;
+	register long x1 __asm__("x1") = b;
+	register long x2 __asm__("x2") = c;
+	register long x3 __asm__("x3") = d;
+	register long x4 __asm__("x4") = e;
+	register long x5 __asm__("x5") = f;
+	register long x6 __asm__("x6") = g;
+	__asm_syscall("r"(x8), "0"(x0), "r"(x1), "r"(x2), "r"(x3), "r"(x4), "r"(x5), "r"(x6));
 }
 
